# Choosing fragments well

Fragments are the units through which a literate program explains itself. Choosing
them well matters more than making them uniformly small. A fragment should express
one idea that the surrounding prose can motivate, name, and connect to a larger
whole.

A useful fragment often answers one question:

- How do we validate this request?
- What belongs in a project snapshot?
- How is a generated position mapped back to its source?
- When must stale work be rejected?
- What makes an output safe to replace?

These questions describe responsibilities rather than source-language shapes. A
class, function, loop, or interface may coincide with one responsibility, but
syntax alone does not determine the boundary.

## Names carry part of the explanation

A fragment name should fit naturally into prose. Verb phrases suit behavior:
`capture the project snapshot`, `plan every generated output`, or `reject a stale
reply`. Noun phrases suit models and declarations: `the parsed project model` or
`language-server capabilities`.

Names such as `helpers`, `state`, `part two`, or `remaining methods` reveal little.
They tell us where code was stored, not why it belongs together. If a fragment
cannot be given a meaningful name, that is evidence that its boundary may be
mechanical rather than conceptual.

Sibling names should also use parallel grammar. The following parent reads as a
sequence of actions:

```text
\<<process a literate project>>=
    \<<capture the project snapshot>>
    \<<build and validate the fragment graph>>
    \<<plan every generated output>>
    \<<commit the output transaction>>
```

A reader can understand the broad algorithm before opening any child. This is one
of the most valuable forms of abstraction in a literate program: the parent is
both executable composition and a compact explanation.

## One idea is not one fixed size

Line counts are useful warnings, not definitions. A sixty-line fragment deserves
careful review because it may hide several decisions. Splitting it every twenty
lines would not necessarily help. The better questions are whether its statements
serve one idea and whether smaller named responsibilities would clarify the parent.

The opposite problem is also possible. A one-line fragment that merely wraps a
function call may force the reader to follow a link without gaining a concept.
Too many fragments can turn a direct explanation into a scavenger hunt.

```NOTE
A useful warning sign is a short passage that asks the reader to follow several
fragment links before any one idea becomes clear. Navigation is not understanding.
```

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Start[Candidate piece of code] --> One{One explainable idea?}
    One -->|No| Divide[Find its distinct responsibilities]
    One -->|Yes| Name{Can it be named naturally?}
    Name -->|No| Rethink[Reconsider the boundary]
    Name -->|Yes| Parent{Does separation clarify its parent?}
    Parent -->|No| Inline[Keep it with the surrounding idea]
    Parent -->|Yes| Kind{Conceptual or mechanical?}
    Kind -->|Conceptual| Main[Explain it in the main chapter]
    Kind -->|Mechanical| Appendix[Place it in a themed appendix]
```

The diagram is a review aid, not a rigid algorithm. Judgment still depends on the
reader, the subject, and the complexity of the implementation.

## Contributions should follow the narrative

A fragment family may be developed in several contributions. This is useful when
the explanation genuinely returns to an earlier idea. A basic parser may be
introduced first and extended later when error recovery becomes relevant.

Contributions should not be used merely to avoid moving code or to scatter one
responsibility across convenient locations. Each return to a fragment should make
sense in the narrative and preserve a deterministic contribution order.

A parent fragment that refers to several families should read coherently. If the
references mix forms such as `settings`, `do validation`, `helper part`, and
`remaining setup`, the composition is probably following source layout rather
than explanation.

## Keep mechanical work visible but secondary

Imports, exports, registration lists, complete-file roots, browser entry points,
and build adapters are necessary. Hiding them would make the book incomplete and
harder to audit. Giving each of them a prominent place in the main argument would
make the book harder to read.

The appendices preserve these mechanisms in themed maps. A reader interested in
how a generated file is assembled can follow them. The main chapter can begin
instead with the problem, model, and decisions that make the file worth having.

This distinction is not based on whether code is difficult. A subtle validation
algorithm may belong in the main text because it expresses the architecture. A
simple registration call may belong in an appendix because it only connects ideas
already explained elsewhere.

## Review the prose and code together

Before accepting a fragment, ask:

1. What idea does it realize?
2. Has the prose explained why that idea exists?
3. Can its name participate naturally in a sentence?
4. Do sibling names form a readable outline?
5. Can a reader skip the code and retain the argument?
6. Would moving it to an appendix remove anything important from that argument?

A literate program cannot answer these questions once and remain finished. As the
architecture changes, prose, diagrams, names, and boundaries must change with it.
The next chapter gives the intuitive fragment model a more precise form and shows
how explanation becomes a deterministic source plan.
